body:
Assuming #90082 will be merged anytime in the foreseeable future, that should fix my gripes. And like @a-johnston said, there should be absolutely no reason why ``old_capacity == 0`` would ever be true (unless things _**really**_ go sideways), so I assume it was put in to stop the compiler from complaining about a possible invalid index? That's the only sane reason I can think of as to why it's there.

This is also another solution for the current codebase. (Comments for clarity)

```cpp
void _resize_and_rehash(uint32_t p_new_capacity_index) {
    // Get the current capacity
    uint32_t old_capacity = hash_table_size_primes[capacity_index];

    // Capacity can't be 0.
    capacity_index = MAX((uint32_t)MIN_CAPACITY_INDEX, p_new_capacity_index);

    // Get the new capacity we should be using
    uint32_t capacity = hash_table_size_primes[capacity_index];

    // Make local copies of the elements and hashes to put into the resized HashMap
    HashMapElement<TKey, TValue> **old_elements = elements;
    uint32_t *old_hashes = hashes;

    // This should never be false, but guard the data of the hashmap if the world is burning
    if(old_capacity > 0) {
        // Reset our internal counts of things
        num_elements = 0;

        // Get a bigger chunk of memory for the resize
        hashes = reinterpret_cast<uint32_t *>(Memory::alloc_static(sizeof(uint32_t) * capacity));
        elements = reinterpret_cast<HashMapElement<TKey, TValue> **>(Memory::alloc_static(sizeof(HashMapElement<TKey, TValue> *) * capacity));

        // Clear the chunk of memory we have for this resize
        for (uint32_t i = 0; i < capacity; i++) {
	    hashes[i] = 0;
	    elements[i] = nullptr;
        }

        // Move the old elements and hashes into the new memory
        for (uint32_t i = 0; i < old_capacity; i++) {
	    if (old_hashes[i] == EMPTY_HASH) {
	        continue;
	    }

	    _insert_with_hash(old_hashes[i], old_elements[i]);
        }

        // Free the old memory
        Memory::free_static(old_elements);
        Memory::free_static(old_hashes);
    }
}
```

This does, realistically, the same exact thing, but puts allocating the new memory and freeing the old memory into the same scope without a chance to exit the scope, preventing any possible leaks from happening. It also guards destroying any data that _**could**_ be in the HashMap if the impossible happens, and does the same thing with preventing the compiler about complaining of invalid indexes, if that's why this check was put into the code in the first place.
